{"id":"cs-615265","type":"UPDATE","payload":{"description":"Spec: `spec.md` §6.\n\n- [ ] Find the app that owns the window under the pointer (not the frontmost app)\n- [ ] Convert the sampled value from extended Display P3 to that app's target profile\n- [ ] Built-in mappings: browsers and Figma → sRGB; Xcode, Sketch, Pixelmator Pro, Preview → Display P3\n- [ ] Settings ▸ Profiles: add, edit and remove app → profile rows; fallback profile (Display P3, sRGB, Generic RGB; default sRGB)\n- [ ] The loupe label shows a badge with the profile it used","dependencies":null,"labels":null},"created_at":"2026-10-15T13:15:35.236501Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.300 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"cs-615265","type":"COMMENT","payload":{"id":"cmt-5de7d384-ec18-4307-960c-92ac0b69171a","text":"Implementation ready for review.\n\n**Core (TDD, 485 tests green)**\n- `SmartProfiles.swift`: `TargetProfile` (sRGB / P3 / Generic RGB, badges), `ProfileRule`, `SmartProfiles` (built-in defaults, case-insensitive bundle-ID lookup, set/remove/restore, lenient decoding).\n- `AppSettings.profiles` is stored in full in `settings.json`.\n- `PickerWindow` + `PickerGeometry.window(at:in:)` find the front window under the point.\n- `PickerSession` takes `windows` and `profiles`. `centerColor` and picks are converted to the profile of the window under the pointer, unclamped. `PickerLabel.profile` carries the badge.\n- Profiles pane is now visible (`hasContent` gate removed).\n\n**App**\n- `PickerController` takes a snapshot of other apps' on-screen windows (`CGWindowList`, desktop excluded, own PID and alpha-0 windows skipped) once per session.\n- The loupe label shows the badge after the value.\n- New `ProfilesPane`: fallback picker, rows with a profile picker and a remove button, an \"Add App\" menu (running apps + Choose…), and Restore Defaults.\n\n**Decisions (agreed):** values outside the target gamut are kept unclamped. The full table is stored. \"Display native\" is dropped per `spec.md` §6, and the issue checklist is updated.\n**Not in the agreed spec:** each row has its own remove button, where the spec planned select + \"−\" (spec updated). New rows start on the fallback profile.\n\n**Verify by hand:** window hit-testing, the badge and the Profiles pane can't be unit-tested. Hover over Safari vs Xcode vs the desktop and check the badge and the stored `colorSpace`. Add, change and remove rows and check `settings.json`."},"created_at":"2026-10-15T13:19:28.811764Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.300 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"cs-48e6e4","type":"CREATE","payload":{"title":"Opening the picker deactivates the frontmost app","description":"Found while testing cs-615265.\n\n`PickerController.open()` calls `NSApp.activate()` and makes an overlay panel key. The previously frontmost app becomes inactive, so many apps switch to their inactive appearance (grey selection, dimmed controls), and the picker samples those colors.\n\n**Cause:** activation is needed today only so `NSCursor.hide()` works: macOS ignores cursor changes from a background app. The overlay panels are already `.nonactivatingPanel`, so they can become key and get keyboard events without activating Colorsip.\n\n**Fix:** stop activating Colorsip. Make the overlay key without activation, and hide the cursor while Colorsip stays in the background.","status":"DOING","parent_id":"cs-71a6c7","sort_order":"a2","dependencies":[{"source_id":"cs-48e6e4","target_id":"cs-615265","kind":"relates_to"}],"labels":["bug"]},"created_at":"2026-10-15T13:27:16.109839Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.300 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"cs-48e6e4","type":"COMMENT","payload":{"id":"cmt-39541667-053b-4631-8928-eb38414adb46","text":"Fixed in the cs-615265 worktree.\n- `PickerController.open()` no longer calls `NSApp.activate()`. The `.nonactivatingPanel` overlay is still made key, so it still gets the keyboard and mouse.\n- New `BackgroundCursor` sets the private `SetsCursorInBackground` WindowServer property (user approved) and hides the cursor with `CGDisplayHideCursor`/`CGDisplayShowCursor`, so it hides while Colorsip stays in the background.\n\nCan't be unit-tested. To verify by hand: in Finder or Xcode, invoke the picker with Hyper+P. The app must stay active (no inactive selection colors), the cursor must hide, and arrows, Esc, Return, scroll and the Option menu must still work."},"created_at":"2026-10-15T13:29:06.929483Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.300 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"cs-48e6e4","type":"COMMENT","payload":{"id":"cmt-9a6aad2b-1fb6-42ac-a2e3-c8fdac1c6058","text":"**The private-API fix crashed WindowServer, so it has been taken out.** On the user's first test, the window server went down and every app was killed. No crash report reached `/Library/Logs/DiagnosticReports`, and the unified log isn't readable from the agent session, so the exact fault isn't confirmed. The prime suspect is the `SetsCursorInBackground` call. It was declared with `@_silgen_name`, which uses the Swift calling convention, not the C one, so the arguments sent to the window server may have been garbage.\n\nCurrent state in the worktree: `BackgroundCursor.swift` is deleted. The picker still doesn't activate Colorsip, and it uses the plain `NSCursor.hide()`/`unhide()` again; these do nothing while Colorsip is in the background. App builds. Not tested in the app yet."},"created_at":"2026-10-15T13:32:23.808289Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.300 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
